    <string name="why_hidden_single">A %2$d fits only one cell in %1$s: every other empty cell there already sees a %2$d in its row, column or box.</string>
    <string name="why_naked_single">The cell at %1$s sees every digit except %2$d in its row, column and box.</string>
    <string name="why_pointing">In %1$s, every place for a %2$d lies in %3$s. So no other cell of %3$s can be %2$d.</string>
    <string name="why_claiming">In %1$s, every place for a %2$d lies inside %3$s. So no other cell of %3$s can be %2$d.</string>
    <string name="why_naked_pair">In %1$s, two cells can only be %2$d or %3$d. They take both, so no other cell there can.</string>
    <string name="why_hidden_pair">The digits %2$d and %3$d fit only the same two cells in %1$s, so those cells hold nothing else.</string>
    <string name="why_x_wing">In %1$s and %2$s, a %5$d fits only where they cross %3$s and %4$s. Each line takes one of those corners, so no other cell of %3$s or %4$s can be %5$d.</string>
